      <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">Are fresh graduates really unhireable?</div><div class="faq-a">No. They're unproven, which looks the same from outside but has a completely different fix. A degree shows you finished a program; it doesn't show an employer you can do their job. Graduates who attach proof of real work stop looking unhireable fast, because the employer can finally see what they can do.</div></div>
      <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">Why can't I get a job with my degree?</div><div class="faq-a">Because the degree stopped being proof. It used to be a shortcut employers trusted; now 85% of them hire on demonstrated skills and around 41.5% of recent grads are underemployed. Your transcript answers a question nobody is asking. The one they are asking, can you do the work, needs evidence.</div></div>
      <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">What actually makes a graduate hireable?</div><div class="faq-a">Proof. A real, scored piece of the work the job involves, that a recruiter can open and judge in seconds. Doing real work is the single biggest protective factor in the data, with internships cutting underemployment risk by nearly half. Evidence beats a list of claims every time.</div></div>
      <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">How fast can I fix it?</div><div class="faq-a">Weeks, because you're not buying another four-year credential, you're producing evidence. A focused person can build and get feedback on a few real work samples in their target role in a month, which is enough to change how every recruiter reads their application.</div></div>
    </section>

